.TH CSORT 7 "2026-10-14" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Overview"
.SH NAME
csort \- overview of the csort stable mergesort
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/csort.h>
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
csort sorts any collection that you can index: a C array, a cvector, a
ring buffer or a container of your own. It is an iterative, bottom-up
mergesort, so there is no recursion and a large input cannot overflow
the stack.

csort_sort(3) does the sorting. It reaches the elements through a getter
function that gives a pointer to the element at an index, and it orders
them with a comparison function that has the contract of qsort(3). If you
don't want to write the comparison yourself,
csort_get_default_comparison_proc(3) picks the one that the library
provides for the type of an expression.

cvec_sort(3) and cvector_sort_with_comparison_proc(3) run the same
mergesort directly on the contiguous buffer of a vector.
.fi
.SS "Guarantees"
.nf
The sort is stable, so elements that compare equal keep their relative
order. It takes O(n log n) time whatever the input, and it allocates one
temporary merge buffer of length * elem_size bytes once it has checked
that product for an overflow.

The elements don't have to be contiguous. The sort reads and writes each
element through the pointer that the getter gives, and each of those
pointers must stay valid for the whole sort.

A call either sorts the whole collection or leaves it completely
unchanged, because every check that can fail runs before the first
element moves. Those checks are a length above the maximum element count
of the library (2^63 on a 64-bit size_t), an mprocs that is not NULL but
does not hold all four of malloc, free, calloc and realloc, an overflow
of length * elem_size, and a failed allocation of the merge buffer.
.fi
.SS "Default comparisons"
.nf
csort_get_default_comparison_proc(3) knows every standard integral
type, bool (false before true), float, double and long double, an
enumerated type (through the integer type it is compatible with), and a
pointer to any character type, which it compares as a NUL-terminated
string. For every other type it gives NULL, a fixed-size char array
included, and you have to write your own comparison for such an element.

An integral type compares three-way, as (a > b) - (a < b). A NaN is
greater than every value that is not NaN and equal only to another NaN,
so a floating-point sort is always a real total order. A NULL string
comes before every string that is not NULL. Plain char, signed char and
unsigned char each have a comparison of their own, which keeps a signed
comparison signed on every platform.

Every default comparison reads its two operands byte by byte, so the
address of an operand needs neither the alignment nor the effective type
of the element.
.fi
.SS "Errors"
.nf
csort_sort(3) gives true on success, and also for a call that has
nothing to do: a NULL collection, a length of 0 or 1, or an elem_size of
0. It gives false for each of the failures listed above. A NULL getter
or a NULL comparison function, for a collection that is not NULL and has
2 or more elements, is a programming error, and the call asserts and
stops the process.
.fi
.SS "Memory management"
.nf
The merge buffer comes from the mprocs that you pass, or from malloc(3)
and free(3) when mprocs is NULL, and the sort frees it before it
returns.
.fi
.SH "THREAD SAFETY"
.nf
csort keeps no state between calls and takes no lock, so several
threads can sort different collections at the same time. You must make
sure that no other thread changes a collection while it is being
sorted. The getter and the comparison function run on the calling
thread.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR csort_sort (3),
.BR csort_get_default_comparison_proc (3),
.BR cvec_sort (3),
.BR cvector_sort_with_comparison_proc (3),
.BR ccollections (7)
